{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"start","type":"text","text":"⭐ The Poet's Beard\n---\nA Story of Art, Betrayal, and Two Italian Anarchists.","x":0,"y":-500,"width":480,"height":120,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"title_main","type":"text","text":"⏰ October 2019\n---\nThe late afternoon sun slants through the parabolic roof, turning the plywood ribs into gold. I stand in the gallery, my blue curls catching the light, breathing in the scent of raw wood and aged canvas.","x":-1600,"y":-3080,"width":440,"height":220,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"shahn_gandhi","type":"text","text":"Behind me, Ben Shahn's \"Gandhi\" watches from the wall — those sad eyes following me as they must have followed him through every FBI interview, every blacklist, every summons to testify.\n\nThe air is thick with something heavier than dust. It's memory, Chadt. It's pain that never left.","x":-1600,"y":-2720,"width":440,"height":340,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"year_matters","type":"text","text":"This is 2019. October. The trees outside are burning with autumn — copper, rust, gold. But the year that matters here is 1959, Chadt.\n---\nThat is when they called Ben Shahn before the committee. That is when he stood in a room full of men in Washington and refused to give up names.","x":-1600,"y":-2240,"width":440,"height":320,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"nakashima_intro","type":"text","text":"Eight years from now, in 1967, a Japanese man will build a gallery in the woods of Pennsylvania. He will bend plywood into impossible shapes, lay concrete and stone with his own hands, and he will fill that gallery with Ben Shahn's paintings.\n---\nThat man was interned in a camp for being Japanese. They took his home, his freedom, his name. His name was George Nakashima. And he built the Arts Building as sanctuary for an artist America tried to silence.","x":-1000,"y":-3080,"width":500,"height":380,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"arts_building","type":"text","text":"We are there now. The Arts Building. October 2019. Outside, the Pennsylvania woods are turning gold and rust.\n---\nThe parabolic roof arches above us — plywood curved like a shell, like a wing — Nakashima built this himself, piece by piece, in 1967.\n\nAnd there, Chadt — on those walls — Shahn's paintings watch us. The colors are still bright. The faces still sad.","x":-1000,"y":-2600,"width":500,"height":360,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"sacco_vanzetti_intro","type":"text","text":"He painted the passion of Sacco and Vanzetti, you know. Two Italian immigrants America murdered.\n---\nHe painted their faces over and over.","x":-400,"y":-3080,"width":400,"height":180,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"sacco_vanzetti_story","type":"text","text":"Nicola Sacco and Bartolomeo Vanzetti. Two Italian anarchists, immigrants, working men.\n---\nThey were accused of murdering a paymaster and his guard in Massachusetts in 1920. They were not guilty, Chadt. Everyone knew it. The evidence was garbage.\n\nBut they were Italian. They were anarchists. They believed in a world without bosses and kings. So in 1927, Massachusetts strapped them to the chair and killed them.","x":-400,"y":-2800,"width":460,"height":420,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"twentythree_times","type":"text","text":"Ben Shahn painted their faces twenty-three times. He could not stop.\n---\nEvery brushstroke was a cry into the void.","x":200,"y":-2340,"width":360,"height":180,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"the_question","type":"text","text":"That is the question, Chadt. That is exactly the question.\n---\nThe real killer was never found — or rather, others were suspected. A circle of anarchists in Massachusetts, followers of a man named Luigi Galleani. They dealt in dynamite. They committed bombings.","x":200,"y":-2060,"width":360,"height":280,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"the_leak_explain","type":"text","text":"But the police did not want them — they wanted Italians. They wanted anarchists. They wanted to make an example.\n---\nSo they grabbed Sacco and Vanzetti, and the real murderer walked free.\n\nThat is the Leak, Chadt. That is the wound in the world. An innocent man dies while the guilty one lives.","x":-400,"y":-1660,"width":400,"height":320,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"april_15_1920","type":"text","text":"⏰ April 15, 1920. South Braintree, Massachusetts.\n---\nThe Slater and Morrill Shoe Factory. 7:30 PM.\n\nTwo men in a factory payroll office. Alessandro Berardelli — a guard — is counting money.\n\nA shot rings out. Then another. Berardelli is dead. The money is gone. Two men are seen running.","x":0,"y":-3080,"width":420,"height":300,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"caught_two_weeks","type":"text","text":"They are caught two weeks later in Philadelphia.\n---\nOne is Nicola Sacco — a fish peddler. One is Bartolomeo Vanzetti — a watermelon vendor.\n\nThey have no alibis. They have thick accents. They have anarchist pamphlets in their pockets.\n\nThat is enough. That is always enough.","x":0,"y":-2660,"width":420,"height":300,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"no_money","type":"text","text":"The money was never recovered. Not a dollar. Not a cent.\n---\nAnd three weeks before the robbery, a man named Carlos Ferrandini was seen casing the factory — except that name was made up.","x":0,"y":-2260,"width":420,"height":200,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"morelli_gang","type":"text","text":"The real gang was the Morelli gang. Joe Morelli and his brothers — Mike, Patsy, Butsy, Fred.\n---\nItalian criminals from Rhode Island. They robbed freight trains. They had a blue Buick.","x":0,"y":-1980,"width":420,"height":180,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"celestino","type":"text","text":"One of them — Celestino Madeiros — he was in jail with Shahn.\n---\nIn 1925 he wrote a note saying: *I was there. It was us. Sacco and Vanzetti are innocent.*\n\nThe prosecution said he was lying. The judge said it did not matter.","x":0,"y":-1720,"width":420,"height":220,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"january_1959","type":"text","text":"⏰ January 1959. Washington D.C., Capitol Hill.\n---\nThe walls are green marble. The air tastes like stale coffee and fear. Ben Shahn sits at a wooden table, sixty years old, his thick hands curled into fists.\n\nMen in suits stare at him. They want names. They want him to tell them who else painted with a brush and a conscience.","x":-2800,"y":300,"width":440,"height":300,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"no_names","type":"text","text":"He had no names to give — or he would not give them. Either way, it was the same.\n---\nThey killed him slowly, over twenty years.","x":-2200,"y":700,"width":380,"height":180,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"watched_house","type":"text","text":"They watched his house, Chadt.\n---\nThere was a car on the corner that never moved. Someone in the house always looked at the phone before they spoke. Two decades of that.\n\nYou learn to swallow your words. You learn to paint quieter. And eventually you learn they want you quieter.","x":-2200,"y":970,"width":380,"height":300,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"seven_paintings","type":"text","text":"After the committee, he made seven paintings in six years.\n---\nSeven. That is all America allowed him.\n\nSeven paintings — and then this building. This is the first wall he has painted in six years where the light comes through the ceiling and not through a government window.","x":-2800,"y":1320,"width":420,"height":280,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"nakashima_sixty_two","type":"text","text":"👥 George Nakashima\n---\nGeorge Nakashima is sixty-two in 1967. He has been in this country for twenty-seven years now since they released him from the camp.\n\nHis hair is grey. His hands are calloused solid wood. He builds furniture every day with his daughter Mira beside him.","x":3000,"y":80,"width":440,"height":320,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"twelve_buildings","type":"text","text":"He has built twelve buildings on this twelve-acre compound — all by hand, all facing south to catch the sun.\n---\nHe is quiet. He smiles rarely. But when he talks about wood — about the grain, the voice inside the tree — his eyes light up like a child seeing snow for the first time.","x":3000,"y":480,"width":440,"height":280,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"wrote_to_ben","type":"text","text":"He wrote to Ben in 1965, offered him the building. And Ben wept.\n---\nHe had nowhere to show his work. Nobody wanted those paintings, those faces that stared at injustice.\n\nThe Arts Building gave them a home. George gave them light, and a roof, and walls that would not betray them.","x":3000,"y":840,"width":440,"height":280,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"mosaic_title","type":"text","text":"🕋 The Poet's Beard\n---\nThe mosaic on the west wall — it is called The Poet's Beard.","x":-400,"y":340,"width":440,"height":140,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"mosaic_explain","type":"text","text":"Ben designed it in a hospital bed in New Jersey. Bernadette held the paper while he pointed.\n---\nThey fired it in French kilns and shipped it across the ocean. It cost more than he made in a year. It is the last thing people see when they leave this building.","x":-400,"y":560,"width":440,"height":240,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"the_poet_figure","type":"text","text":"He designed it from a figure in his earlier lithograph — *The Poet*.\n---\nThe Beard flows down like water, like moss, like time. It is every word they were not allowed to say.\n\nIt is a forest. It is a river. It is silence forced upon them. The tiles are the words they swallowed.","x":-400,"y":880,"width":440,"height":280,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"the_lithograph","type":"text","text":"Look at the lithograph — the hands are covering the mouth.\n---\nThat is what they did to him. That is what they did to every one of us.","x":-400,"y":1240,"width":440,"height":180,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"two_men_title","type":"text","text":"👥 Two men behind it\n---\nFrederick Katzmann — the District Attorney. Harvard-educated, young, hungry, ambitious. He wanted to be governor.\n\nThat case made him famous — that was the point.","x":0,"y":2540,"width":420,"height":200,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"thayer","type":"text","text":"And Judge Webster Thayer — he asked to be assigned to the trial.\n---\nHe had given a speech to new citizens calling anarchists a threat. During the trial he told the jury: *You wait till I give my charge. I will show them.*\n\nAnd he did. Both men walked away from it. Thayer got a raise. Katzmann got a private practice.","x":0,"y":2820,"width":420,"height":280,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"negligence","type":"text","text":"Negligence? No. Intent.\n---\nIt was a message: *Kill the anarchists and the immigrants will get the point.*","x":0,"y":3180,"width":420,"height":140,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"letter_one","type":"text","text":"⏰ December 28, 1924 — Sacco to Vanzetti from Dedham Jail:\n---\n*I hope that we will see each other soon. You are quite right when you say — after all we are still on our feet — because we always keep in our soul the hope and faithful in our innocent.*\n\n*I am sure that we will keep this hope and faithful till the bright day of our triumph.*\n\nHe signs it: *Your faithful comrade now and forever, Ferdinando Sacco.*","x":3000,"y":2580,"width":440,"height":340,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"letter_two","type":"text","text":"⏰ Another — Sacco to his daughter Ines, July 1927:\n---\n*I love you so much as you are the dearest little beloved one. I long so much to have you hear all the heartbeat eagerness of your father.*","x":3000,"y":2980,"width":440,"height":180,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"letter_three","type":"text","text":"⏰ Vanzetti — last letter, August 1927, from the death house:\n---\n*We are innocent. This is a war of plutocracy against liberty. We die for Anarchy. Long life Anarchy.*","x":3000,"y":3220,"width":440,"height":180,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"nicola","type":"text","text":"👥 Nicola — Nico\n---\nNicola — Nico — was quiet. He kept to himself. A shoemaker by trade — steady hands, quiet hands.\n\nHe was handsome in a quiet way — people said so. He loved his wife Rosaria and their daughter Ines and son Dante.\n\nHe was the kind of man who would work twelve hours and then read anarchist pamphlets by candlelight. He was serious. He did not smile easily. But when he did, people remembered.","x":0,"y":2600,"width":420,"height":320,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"bartolomeo","type":"text","text":"👥 Bartolomeo — Bartolo\n---\nBartolomeo — Bartolo — was the opposite. He was a fish peddler — he walked through Plymouth *shouting* fish, calling out. He was loud, warm, a talker. He made people laugh.\n\nHe could sell ice to an Eskimo and then argue philosophy with them after. He was the one who gave speeches, who organized the strikes. He was short, wiry, always moving.","x":0,"y":3000,"width":420,"height":320,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"opposites","type":"text","text":"Together they were opposites that made a whole. The quiet one and the loud one. The hands and the voice.\n---\nAnd in the cell they learned to be both in one.\n\nThey did not choose each other. They met in a cell. They learned each other in seven years of waiting. And when they walked to the chair, they did not let go.","x":0,"y":3420,"width":420,"height":280,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"not_love","type":"text","text":"That is not love as poems write it.\n---\nThat is love as dying makes it.","x":0,"y":3780,"width":420,"height":120,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"ritual_text","type":"text","text":"🏁 The Ritual\n---\n*In this place, to the meter,*\n*At this time, to the hour,*\n*We seal the Leak, to thy name:*\n\n**Webster Thayer.**","x":1320,"y":2620,"width":440,"height":320,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"epilogue_2019","type":"text","text":"⏰ 2019. The paintings are still here, Chadt.\n---\nThey are still watching. The Poet's Beard cracks in one place but it is repaired. Mira — George's daughter — she runs the foundation.\n\nTourists come. They stand where we stood. They read the plaque. They learn the names: Sacco and Vanzetti.","x":1320,"y":-360,"width":400,"height":260,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"crate_away","type":"text","text":"But they have crated most of Shahns work, stored it away in the dark.\n---\nOnly the mosaic and the large mural remain. They said it was conservation. They said it was preservation.","x":1320,"y":-20,"width":400,"height":180,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"subversive","type":"text","text":"The subversive ones — they are still out there, being erased.\n---\nWe just have to know where to look.","x":1320,"y":220,"width":400,"height":140,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"end","type":"text","text":"🏁 The Leak is sealed.\n---\nThe wound is closed. But memory — memory is the only thing that lasts.","x":1320,"y":440,"width":400,"height":140,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"location_detail","type":"text","text":"📍 Slater & Morrill Shoe Factory\nPearl Street, South Braintree, Massachusetts\n\nApril 15, 1920, 3:05 PM","x":1800,"y":-3080,"width":340,"height":180},
		{"id":"location_today","type":"text","text":"The site today:\n---\nThe factory was demolished in 1975. Today the Pearl Plaza shopping center stands there.\n\nA memorial was placed at 1 French Avenue in Braintree in 2010.","x":1800,"y":-2800,"width":340,"height":180},
		{"id":"shahn_born","type":"text","text":"👥 Ben Shahn\n---\nHe was born in Kaunas. Russian Empire then. 1898.\n\nHis father scrubbed floors for Americans who looked through him. They came through Ellis Island like everyone else. He was eight years old.","x":-2800,"y":-2800,"width":400,"height":220},
		{"id":"shahn_worked","type":"text","text":"He did not paint then — he worked. A button factory at twelve. A printing shop at fourteen — that is where he first touched ink, Chadt.\n---\nThe black ink running under his fingers, the smell of it, that is the first art he knew.","x":-2800,"y":-2480,"width":400,"height":220},
		{"id":"first_time","type":"text","text":"The first time was 1930.\n---\nA gallery in Manhattan took three of his paintings. He walked out onto Forty-Ninth Street and he was crying, Chadt. A grown man crying in the street like a child.\n\nHe was thirty-two years old and he was a real painter. I could say the words.","x":-2800,"y":-2160,"width":400,"height":260},
		{"id":"nakashima_interned","type":"text","text":"👥 George Nakashima\n---\nBorn May 24, 1905, Spokane, Washington. MIT-trained architect.\n\nDecember 7, 1941: Pearl Harbor attacked. March 1942: Sent to Minidoka War Relocation Center, Idaho.\n\nWife Marion, baby daughter Mira in adjacent barracks.","x":3500,"y":-80,"width":400,"height":220,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"nakashima_released","type":"text","text":"1943: Released; sponsored by Antonin Raymond. 1945: Established studio in New Hope, Pennsylvania.\n---\nHe built furniture every day with his daughter Mira beside him. He is quiet. He smiles rarely. But when he talks about wood — his eyes light up like a child seeing snow.","x":3500,"y":260,"width":400,"height":220,"color":"6"}
	],
